WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.080
When it comes to the best villains in the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.920
two names are almost always on the&nbsp;
table: Doflamingo and Crocodile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.000
However, who of the two is actually the better&nbsp;
villain? I think there can only be one clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.480
choice here, however there is actually a lot more&nbsp;
to this question than you might think. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.720
what I want to discuss with you today is why&nbsp;
Doflamingo is the better villain and why that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.680
actually not a bad thing for Crocodile at all. Now&nbsp;
you might or might not agree with this statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.080
and so I recommend you watch this video until the&nbsp;
very end so you know what I really mean by that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.080
Oh yeah and before we get into&nbsp;
it, if you haven’t subscribed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.480
to the channel yet, make sure to hit that&nbsp;
big red button below this video.    
 

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.320
If you are interested in storytelling, even&nbsp;
just a little, you will know that within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.240
long-lasting format of One Piece, Oda has been&nbsp;
referencing events, themes and symbols from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.840
before the time skip, after the crew has reached&nbsp;
the new world, basically on a constant basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:34.720
Fishman Island references Arlong Park, Punk Hazard&nbsp;
mirrors Drum Island and then we come to Dressrosa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.480
What does this Arc and the main protagonist&nbsp;
mirror from the first part of the story?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.680
As my good friend mr Morj explains in one of&nbsp;
his fantastic videos, there are actually some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:00.640
interesting similarities between Enel and Dofi,&nbsp;
as both of them have a serious god-complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.960
ruling in tyranny over their people in the&nbsp;
believe that they stand above everyone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:12.800
and ultimately are taken down by god’s natural&nbsp;
enemy: Luffy. And I definitely agree with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:19.200
parallel and believe that Oda has integrated&nbsp;
parts of Enel into Dofi. However, going not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.520
only by the order of pre-time skip arcs, but also&nbsp;
by the vast number of parallels between the two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:33.520
the main inspiration for Dressrosa and Doflamingo&nbsp;
was without any doubt Alabasta and yes, no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:41.680
ales than Crocodile himself. In fact, the list of&nbsp;
similarities between the two warlords is so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:49.840
that I will move through it fairly quickly&nbsp;
here to not make this video unnecessarily long.&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.840
Let’s start with the more obvious parallels and&nbsp;
then move a bit deeper. On the most basic level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.560
both the second saga of pre-time skip, Alabasta,&nbsp;
and the second saga of post-time skip, Dressrosa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.720
are about a Kingdom, that is suffering under the&nbsp;
rule of one of the Shijibukai. One controlled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:19.200
a crocodile, the other by a flamingo, going&nbsp;
with the animal theme of the warlords. Also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.040
if you’re like me and for some reason completely&nbsp;
missed this super obvious theme until now, here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:33.760
a quick overview: Crocodile is a Crocodile.&nbsp;
Doflamingo is a flamingo. Mihawk is a hawk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.680
Kuma literally means bear in Japanese. Boa&nbsp;
Hancock is of course a boa constrictor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:51.360
Jinbe means whale shark in Japanese. And&nbsp;
finally, Gecko Moria has actually two animals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:59.840
There’s the gecko, but putting his name together,&nbsp;
Ge-komoria, Komori actually means bat in Japanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.000
There, if you weren’t aware&nbsp;
of that yet, now you know.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.800
Getting back to topic, Doflamingo and Crocodile&nbsp;
share a lot more than just this superficial theme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.200
Both were the leaders of powerful criminal&nbsp;
organisations with a number of devil fruit users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.840
including a bomber, someone who can change&nbsp;
their weight, someone who can turn their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:34.000
body into weapons, a power related to art&nbsp;
and someone who can move freely underground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.920
Both the Baroque-works as well as the&nbsp;
Donquixote family have themed code-names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.400
one with numbers, the other with playing&nbsp;
cards. As a result, both Mr. 0 and Joker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.880
operated under the façade of celebrated&nbsp;
heroes in their respective countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.800
committing dark dealings that contributed to&nbsp;
the countries' deterioration. Both were trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.280
illegal substances as part of their scheme, Dance&nbsp;
Powder and SAD. And both of them have mastered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:09.040
their devil fruit powers to high levels, able to&nbsp;
severely distort the surrounding environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.320
Both of them at first seem to get decapitated,&nbsp;
Crocodile by Vivi and Dofi by Kyros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.200
both of them easily avoid any harm&nbsp;
however by displaying their skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:37.840
Oh yeah, and for some reason both of them also&nbsp;
seem to have thing for drape furry coats. 

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.400
But this is far from all. Let’s move to a&nbsp;
more personal level now. Even from a plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:53.120
and character perspective, Doflamingo is clearly&nbsp;
referencing Crocodile’s story line. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.840
both were present at Roger’s funeral being&nbsp;
inspired to advance their efforts to reach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:04.480
top of the pirate world. Both aimed to do so by&nbsp;
first establishing control over an entire country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.240
However both of them earned the scorn of the&nbsp;
country’s princess. Both Vivi and Viola went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.600
undercover in their respective organisations&nbsp;
to oppose their evil schemes and protect their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:23.840
father, King Cobra and King Riku. Both of them&nbsp;
see their subordinates as tools to advance their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:30.000
own goals. This can be seen especially well in how&nbsp;
they treat those who do not perform well enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:37.360
Crocodile completely disregarded Galdinao&nbsp;
and Doflamingo lost any interest in Bellamy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:47.840
after his failure against Luffy,&nbsp;
exploiting his admiration for him.    

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.960
And we can take this all the way to&nbsp;
their respective conflicts with Luffy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.320
In both cases it needed Luffy&nbsp;
to uncover their true face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.880
and liberate the country form their tyranny. At&nbsp;
first, both Crocodile and Dofi seem superior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:13.200
Luffy and in both cases Luffy manages to take&nbsp;
them out in the end anyway. Also the way Luffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.880
destroys Doflamingo's string clone is similar&nbsp;
to Crocodile's defeat, as they both are sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.400
up to the ceiling and were beaten up by an attack&nbsp;
which involves Luffy punching them multiple times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.520
until the ceiling gets destroyed as they&nbsp;
fall to the ground. And ultimately both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.360
them not only lost their status as warlords,&nbsp;
but also ended up in level 6 of impel down.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.680
As you can see, the parallels between both&nbsp;
villains are endless and I think it should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:54.960
self-evident that Doflamingo is strongly inspired&nbsp;
by Crocodile’s role pre-time skip. So now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.720
you might say. ‘Well if Doflamingo is just a copy&nbsp;
of Crocodile, doesn’t that make Crocodile the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:06.640
better villain?’ So let’s talk about why I think&nbsp;
Doflamingo is superior here from a storytelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:13.840
perspective. The most fundamental assumption here&nbsp;
is that good writers improve over the curse of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:20.800
career. They learn what works, what doesn’t, how&nbsp;
to structure things better and improve characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.760
And while for most authors this will happen over&nbsp;
the course over multiple books, screenplays and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:33.600
stories, Oda has been writing the same story for&nbsp;
over 20 years. And so all of his trial and error&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:39.200
as well as all of his experience and improvement&nbsp;
are visible throughout that same story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:46.800
line. So as a result I think that Oda made it a&nbsp;
point to revisit Alabasta, that was already great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:53.840
and wanted to prove that he can make it&nbsp;
even better. A similar setting, but better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.800
Similar story, but better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.920
This doesn’t mean in any way, that I dislike&nbsp;
Alabasta in the slightest. Just the opposite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:17.280
the fact that Alabasta was such a fantastic saga&nbsp;
was the thing that made Dressrosa what it is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:24.640
the first place. Also as one big caveat here, I’m&nbsp;
strictly talking about the manga here. The anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:31.280
adaptation of Dressrosa was in my opinion way&nbsp;
worse than the manga, while the anime did really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:38.160
well with Alabasta. That being said, Dressrosa&nbsp;
is woven way better into the overall flow of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.800
the story and the overarching events than&nbsp;
Alabasta did. Once it was over, it basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:51.440
felt a bit like a hard cut. Similarly, Dressrosa&nbsp;
integrates many of the major themes of the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:59.840
little better than Alabasta does. The helplessness&nbsp;
of the Marines in Dressrosa has more weight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.360
Fujitora’s experience with Luffy happens on a&nbsp;
more fundamental level I think than with smoker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:11.280
who’s transition happens over a longer period&nbsp;
of time. Even an admiral could do nothing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.920
the face of the warlord system, but in&nbsp;
the end Fujitora actually abolished it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:22.800
But moving on to the villains themselves,&nbsp;
I think it’s great that Oda made Doflamingo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:29.680
what Crocodile aspired to be. He wanted to gain&nbsp;
control over the country and build his power from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:36.960
there. Doflamingo actually managed to do so. He&nbsp;
completely overthrew the king and ruled openly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:50.000
As a result, I think that in the final moments&nbsp;
of both battles, the positions of Luffy and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:56.800
villain are extremely important to the symbolism&nbsp;
of the arc and time. Against Crocodile, Luffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:03.280
is below him and defeats by punching him into the&nbsp;
sky, revealing him as the crook and monster he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:09.920
to all of Alabasta by literally throwing him into&nbsp;
the light. Luffy being below him during the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:16.160
moments represents Luffy's climb and eventual&nbsp;
triumph over Crocodile as the underdog, recovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:22.720
from his previous losses. It is a struggle for&nbsp;
Luffy to punch Crocodile into the open and through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:28.080
bedrock, further symbolising the barrier Crocodile&nbsp;
has created to protect himself from the public.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:43.120
Luffy vs Doflamingo on the other hand&nbsp;
contrasts this. Luffy is now&nbsp;above&nbsp;the villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.280
symbolically representing that Luffy&nbsp;
is now the one with the upper hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:52.960
Doflamingo has already been revealed to the&nbsp;
country of Dressrosa as the monster he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:57.280
so they are in the sky and being&nbsp;
watched by everyone on the island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.840
Luffy's final attack against Doffy is a complete&nbsp;
curb stomp, with not much that can be done to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:10.320
it. The ground underneath them being demolished is&nbsp;
symbolic of the foundation of lies the country was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:17.680
built on being crushed. This is further symbolised&nbsp;
in the techniques Luffy uses to defeat them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:24.840
Luffy defeated Crocodile's single attack (Spada)&nbsp;
with a flurry of multiple punches (Storm)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.720
while Doflamingo's multi-pronged attack&nbsp;
(Spider Web + God Thread) was broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:42.320
through with one huge punch (King Kong Gun).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:48.400
I think it is clear that these two scenes are&nbsp;
meant to mirror each other. And so once again Oda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:55.200
uses all the good he did with Crocodile to enhance&nbsp;
Doflamingo. And so, I think that both Crocodile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:02.400
AND Doflamingo are fantastic characters. However,&nbsp;
I think that Dofi is superior in the sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:10.640
Oda was able to build on, improve and expand&nbsp;
what he did with Crocodile. Also, I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:19.840
that Doflamingo’s character overall is one&nbsp;
of the most complex in the story so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:28.480
If you want to learn more about that, I recommend&nbsp;
that you check out my character analysis here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:31.280
Thanks guys. Peace.

